(CLI Discrepancy 1 - minimal repro. `--link <libs>` is documented (5152)
 as "Link against shared libraries (comma-separated)", and the worked
 example (5169) shows it used together with `--lib-path`:
 `vox main.vox --link math --lib-path ./libs`. Hand-verified across four
 separate transcripts (same-directory, subdirectory, and a
 two-candidate-library case - CLI-05.vox carries all four) that omitting
 `--link` never once changes the outcome: the `see <lib> version <v>
 from "<path>.lib"` statement itself already names the exact `.lib` file
 to consume, and `--lib-path` alone is what's actually load-bearing (it
 controls where that file is searched for; CLI-06.vox). This file is the
 single side-by-side minimal case: identical source, compiled twice,
 with and without --link, from the same directory as its library.

 The reading in which the compiler is correct: `--link`'s job may be for
 linking against a foreign (non-Vox, e.g. hand-assembled) consumer that
 never runs the `see ... from ".lib"` path at all - LANGUAGE.md's own
 Shared Libraries section (out of this ledger's line range) describes a
 `mathkit_driver.asm` that calls into a `.so` directly, with no `.lib`
 interface and hence no compiler-side auto-discovery to piggyback on. If
 that is genuinely the only case where --link does something, this
 section's Options table and worked example both illustrate it with the
 one case (a normal `see`-based Vox consumer) where it demonstrably does
 nothing - which is still a real documentation gap even under this
 reading. Not filed.

 NOT runnable as a single file: needs a prebuilt libtally.so/.lib pair.
 Transcript:
   $ vox tally_lib.vox --shared -o libtally.so
   $ vox tally_user.vox -o with_link --link tally
   $ vox tally_user.vox -o without_link
   $ ./with_link
   42
   $ ./without_link
   42
 Ran: (not runnable as a single file; see transcript above)
)
